\b\f4\fs48\kerning36 Jumpstarting With a SPARC: Netbooting Sun Workstations
\par }\pard \sb199\sa199 {\b\i\f4\fs36 Revision 3.6.2}{\b\f4\fs36 
\par Foreword
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f4 
This document was compiled by me (Europa) in 2024 and contains a combination of information from others that I have placed here for posterity (and updated where applicable) and information based on my own experiences. I would like to thank the man
y administrators and hobbyists that came before me and have made it possible for me to use and learn about my Sun systems in a manner that allows me to create this document through the recording of their knowledge. I would also like to specifically thank 
NCommander and their community for helping me when limited documentation was available. I hope this document will survive for years to come and be passed from one hand to the next. Archival and documentation is important, and I hope that this document aid
s with that effort.
\par }{\i\f4 
A note about inline citations: As I said, some of this guide is taken from other sources. To streamline citation of those sources, I have put letters in parentheses next to the appropriate section headings. These correspond to webpages referenced in Appen
dix C. I do not wish to take credit for the work of others, and I believe firmly in crediting sources.}{\f4 
\par }\pard \sb199\sa199 {\b\f4\fs36 Abstract
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f4 If you're reading this document, it's likely that you have been brought here from a web search or someone linked it as an answe
r to a question you or someone else posed. The purpose of this document is, first and foremost, to provide a consolidated resource for those looking to boot their Sun workstations from the network based on my findings. There are many reasons one may wish 
to do this, including a lack of physical installation media or a lack of working removable media drives on the system one wishes to boot.
\par This document will make a few assumptions, namely that you have a basic understanding of networking and Unix-like opera
ting systems, as well as assuming that you have root access (either directly or via su) to the server system, and that you'll be working from a root shell. As I am limited by the hardware I have at my disposal, the procedures listed in this document have 
been tested on a SPARCstation 5, an Ultra 1, and an Ultra 5. That being said, the basics of this document should hold true for any Sun system that can boot from the network in the same way that these systems can. I will primarily focus on booting from Net
BSD (and OpenBSD where applicable) and Solaris. If you wish to apply these instructions to other platforms and operating systems, I have included the resources I pulled from as well as other useful resources in Appendix C.
\par Before you proceed, you must have a few things prepared:
\par }\pard \fi-360\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 A wired network. It does not need to be connected to the internet, although it may be beneficial for some client operating systems.
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 A system that can act as a server and a system that you wish to be your client. The server can be anything capable of wired networking and running an operating system capable of performing the tasks in this guide.
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 Your client's platform group. If you don't know this, please refer to Appendix B-i.
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 
A programmed IDPROM and NVRAM on the client. Unless you have specifically ensured your timekeeper's battery has juice left, it's likely dead. If you need to program your timekeeper, refer to Appendix A. If you have all of those things, feel free to procee
d to whichever section is applicable to your goals. E
very network and configuration is different, and you may have to tweak these guides to meet your needs. I can say these worked on my machines but, as always with things like this, your mileage may vary. I make no guarantees about any of the stuff here oth
er than that it has been tested by me to work. I, however, am an individual and only have so many resources at my disposal, so I cannot test every possible configuration. I will add more configurations with server and client operating systems as I test th
em and, if anyone has their own configurations that they would like to add, feel free to do so on Github. Have fun and best of luck.
\par }\pard \sb199\sa199 {\b\f4\fs36 I. SunOS 4 Diskless Install
\par }{\b\i\f4\fs36 I-a. NetBSD/OpenBSD}{\b\f4\fs36  }{\b\f4\fs36\super 1}{\b\f4\fs36 
\par }\pard \fi-360\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 1.\tab Ensure you have the following services enabled:
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 rarpd
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 bootparams
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 tftpd
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 bootps or dhcpd
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 nfs services
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 2.\tab These services are also in /etc/inetd.conf and may be useful to uncomment (IF you are on a trusted network):
\par }\pard \fi-360\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 ftp
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 telnet
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 rlogin
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 rsh
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 rexec
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 time
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 3.\tab Create or modify the /etc/ethers file with your Sun's ethernet address and hostname:
\par }\pard \tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656 {\f11\fs20 /etc/ethers:
\par 08:00:20:00:07:EA client
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 4.\tab Modify /etc/hosts with the hostname you specified in /etc/ethers and the IP address you wish to assign your client
\par }\pard \tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656 {\f11\fs20 /etc/hosts:
\par ...
\par 192.168.0.11 client
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 5.\tab Make the /tftpboot directory if it doesn't already exist.
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f11\fs20 # mkdir /tftpboot}{\f4 
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 6.\tab Make a directory where you wish to put the contents of the install CD.
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f11\fs20 # mkdir /home/client}{\f4 
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 7.\tab Create or modify the /etc/bootparams file.
\par }\pard \tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656 {\f11\fs20 /etc/bootparams:
\par installclient root=server:/home/client swap=server:/home/client/swap gateway=server:0xffffff00
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 8.\tab Add the location of the copied install CD to /etc/exports.
\par }\pard \tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656 {\f11\fs20 /etc/exports:
\par /home/client -alldirs -maproot=root
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100\tx720 {\f4 9.\tab Restart (or start) all services listed in Step 1.
\par 10.\tab Mount the SunOS 4.1.x CD
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f11\fs20 # mount -t cd9660 -o ro /dev/cd0 /cdrom}{\f4 
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 11.\tab Set up the client's filesystem heirarchy and swap file.
\par }\pard \tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656 {\f11\fs20 # cd /home/client
\par 
\par # dd if=/dev/zero of=swap bs=<swap size in megabytes>m count=1
\par 
\par # mkdir -p usr/kvm
\par 
\par # tar -xvpf /cdrom/export/exec/proto_root_sunos_4_1_4
\par 
\par `# cd /home/client/usr
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 12.\tab Extract the common OS packages.
\par }\pard \tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656 {\f11\fs20 # for x in /cdrom/export/exec/sun4_sunos_4_1_4/*; do tar -xpf $x; done
\par 
\par # tar -xpf /cdrom/export/share/sunos_4_1_4/manual
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 13.\tab Install the architecture-specific packages. (If you don't know what platform group or architecture your system is, refer to Appendix B-i. For the purposes of demonstration, I'm using sun4m.)
\par }\pard \tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656 {\f11\fs20 # cd kvm
\par 
\par # tar -xpf /cdrom/export/exec/kvm/sun4m_sunos_4_1_4/kvm
\par 
\par # tar -xpf /cdrom/export/exec/kvm/sun4m_sunos_4_1_4/sys
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 14.\tab Add the server and the client to the client's /etc/hosts file.
\par }\pard \tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656 {\f11\fs20 ./etc/hosts:
\par <server ip address> server
\par 192.168.0.11 client
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 15.\tab Create the client's /dev nodes.
\par }\pard \tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656 {\f11\fs20 # cd dev
\par 
\par # sed -i 's|^PATH=|PATH=/sbin:|g' MAKEDEV
\par 
\par # ./MAKEDEV std
\par 
\par # ./MAKEDEV pty0
\par 
\par # ./MAKEDEV win
\par 
\par # cd ..
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 16.\tab Create the client's /etc/fstab file.
\par }\pard \tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656 {\f11\fs20 ./etc/fstab:
\par server:/home/client / nfs rw 0 0
\par server:/home/client/usr /usr nfs rw 0 0
\par swap /tmp tmp rw 0 0
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\i\f4 (Special note: At this time it would be ideal to also uncomment the mount /tmp line in rc.local so that /tmp will be mounted and you can use X11 and OpenWindows, among other things.)}{\f4 
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 17.\tab Copy critical files to sbin and vmunix to the root directory.
\par }\pard \tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656 {\f11\fs20 # cp usr/kvm/boot/* sbin
\par 
\par # cp usr/kvm/stand/sh sbin
\par 
\par # cp usr/bin/hostname sbin
\par 
\par # cp usr/kvm/stand/vmunix .
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 18.\tab Copy the tftp boot file to /tftpboot and symlink it to the proper name for your system. (If you don't know how to do this, refer to Appendix B-iv.)
\par }\pard \tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656 {\f11\fs20 # cp usr/kvm/stand/boot.sun4m /tftpboot
\par 
\par # cd /tftpboot
\par 
\par # ln -s boot.sun4m C0A8000B.SUN4M
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 19.\tab If all works out, at this point you should be able to tell the Sun to boot into single user mode from the network.
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f11\fs20 ok boot net -s}{\f4 
\par }\pard \sb240\sa240 {\b\i\f4\fs28 On the Sun:}{\b\f4\fs28 
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 20.\tab Compile the source for the various utilities in the bigfs patch and put them in their proper places.
\par }\pard \fi-360\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 /etc/dump
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 /etc/fsck
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 /usr/bin/df and /usr/5bin/df
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 /etc/fsirand
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 /etc/mkfs
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 /usr/etc/quotacheck
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\i\f4 (fsusageck.c will fail to compile but it doesn't seem to be necessary, as I don't actually find it anywhere in the system.)}{\f4 
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 21.\tab Apply the NFS Jumbo Patch.
\par }\pard \tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656 {\f11\fs20 # cd /path/to/nfs/patch
\par 
\par # cp /usr/kvm/sys/`arch -k`/OBJ/nfs_client.o /usr/kvm/sys/`arch -k`/OBJ/nfs_client.old
\par 
\par `# cp `arch -k`/nfs_client.o /usr/kvm/sys/\\`arch -k`/OBJ/nfs_client.o
\par 
\par # cp /usr/kvm/sys/`arch -k`/OBJ/nfs_server.o /usr/kvm/sys/`arch -k`/OBJ/nfs_server.old
\par 
\par # cp `arch -k`/nfs_server.o /usr/kvm/sys/`arch -k`/OBJ/nfs_server.o
\par 
\par # cp /usr/kvm/sys/`arch -k`/OBJ/nfs_vnodeops.o /usr/kvm/sys/`arch -k`/OBJ/nfs_vnodeops.old
\par 
\par # cp `arch -k`/nfs_vnodeops.o /usr/kvm/sys/`arch -k`/OBJ/nfs_vnodeops.o
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 22.\tab Recompile the kernel.
\par }\pard \tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656 {\f11\fs20 # cd /sys/`arch -k`/conf
\par 
\par # cp GENERIC GENERIC.1
\par 
\par # config GENERIC.1
\par 
\par # cd ../GENERIC.1
\par 
\par # make
\par 
\par # cp /vmunix /vmunix.save
\par 
\par # cp vmunux /vmunix
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 23.\tab Reboot into multi-user mode this time. If everyt
hing was done correctly it should boot fully and you can login as root without it panicking. (Note: if your default boot device is set to something other than net, you should }{\f11\fs20 halt}{\f4  instead of reboot and then issue the }{\f11\fs20 boot net
}{\f4  command at the OpenBoot "ok" prompt.)
\par }\pard \sb199\sa199 {\b\f4\fs36 II. Solaris Diskful Install
\par }{\b\i\f4\fs36 II-a NetBSD/OpenBSD}{\b\f4\fs36 
\par }\pard \fi-360\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 1.\tab Ensure you have the following services enabled:
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 rarpd
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 bootparams
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 tftpd
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 bootps or dhcpd
\par }{\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab }{\f4 nfs services
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 2.\tab Create a directory where you want the server to place the Solaris install files.
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f11\fs20 # mkdir -p /export/Solaris}{\f4 
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 3.\tab Mount the install CD
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f11\fs20 # mount -o ro /dev/cd0 /mnt}{\f4 
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 4.\tab Copy the contents of the CD to the directory created in step 1
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f11\fs20 # cp -a /mnt/* /export/Solaris/}{\f4 
\par }{\i\f4 Note for Solaris 2.6 and 7: There are hidden files on the root of the CD-ROM that may not be copied by the above command and required for a successful boot of the installer. Run }{\i\f11\fs20 ls -a /export/Solaris}{\i\f4 
 and compare it with the listing output by }{\i\f11\fs20 ls -a /mnt}{\i\f4 . If there are missing hidden files or folders, copy them individually using }{\i\f11\fs20 cp -a}{\i\f4 .}{\f4 
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100\tx720 {\f4 5.\tab I have yet to test this method on multi-disc install sets, please do notify me if you have a verified method of adding a second disc to the install source directory.
\par 6.\tab Create or modify the /etc/ethers file with your Sun's ethernet address and hostname:
\par }\pard \tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656 {\f11\fs20 /etc/ethers:
\par 8:0:20:c0:ff:ee installclient
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 7.\tab Modify /etc/hosts with the hostname you specified in /etc/ethers and the IP address you wish to assign your client
\par }\pard \tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656 {\f11\fs20 /etc/hosts:
\par ...
\par 192.168.0.10 installclient
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 8.\tab Make the /tftpboot directory if it doesn't already exist.
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f11\fs20 # mkdir /tftpboot}{\f4 
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 9.\tab Create or modify the /etc/bootparams file.
\par }\pard \tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656 {\f11\fs20 /etc/bootparams:
\par installclient root=install.server.ip.address:/export/Solaris/Solaris_<version>/Tools/Boot install=install.server.ip.address:/export/Solaris boottype=:in   rootopts=:rsize=32768
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 10.\tab Add the location of the copied install CD to /etc/exports.
\par }\pard \tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656 {\f11\fs20 /etc/exports:
\par /export/Solaris /export/Solaris/Solaris_<version>/Tools/Boot -mapall=root
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 11.\tab 
Copy the tftp booter to /tftpboot and symbolically link the Sun boot file to the /tftpboot directory, where the name is your Sun's IP address represented in hexadecimal followed by your platform group. (If you don't know how to do this, please see Appendi
x B-iv. If you don't know your platform group, please refer to Appendix B-i.)
\par }\pard \tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656 {\f11\fs20 
# cp -p /usr/exports/Solaris/Solaris_<version>/Tools/Boot/usr/platform/<platform group>/lib/fs/nfs/inetboot /tftpboot/inetboot_<platform group>_sol<version>
\par # ln -s  /tftpboot/C0A8000A.<platform group>
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 12.\tab Restart all services listed in Step 1.
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f4 *}{\i\f4 If you enabled tftpd and/or bootpd, be sure to restart inetd.}{\f4 
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 13.\tab If everything went according to plan, you can tell your Sun workstation to boot from the network and it will boot into the Solaris installer.
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f11\fs20 ok boot net}{\f4 
\par }\pard \sb199\sa199 {\b\i\f4\fs36 II-b. Solaris}{\b\f4\fs36  }{\b\f4\fs36\super 2}{\b\f4\fs36 
\par }\pard \fi-360\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 1.\tab Create a directory where you want the server to place the Solaris install files.
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f11\fs20 # mkdir /var/Solaris}{\f4 
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 2.\tab Navigate to the Tools directory on the install disc.
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f11\fs20 # cd /path/to/Tools}{\f4 
\par }\pard \fi-360\li720\sb120\sa120\tx720 {\f4 3.\tab Run the setup_install_server script and direct it to the directory created in Step 1
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f11\fs20 # ./setup_install_server /var/Solaris}{\f4 
