\par }{\b\i\f4\fs36 A-i. What is the NVRAM, IDPROM, hostid, and ethernet address, and why is it important?}{\b\f4\fs36 
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f4 
Before proceeding, a warning: improper use of the OpenBoot PROM console can brick your system. I know the information contained in this document to be correct, but I cannot guarantee you won't brick your machine if you go beyond what I talk about here. Pr
oceed with caution. This is not to scare you, but to inform you.
\par Your Sun's NVRAM and IDPROM hold firmware configuration settings. This includes the default boot device, diagnostic behavior, default display device, and, perhaps most importantly for this guide, your ethernet address and hostid.
\par If you're reading this section of the guide (and, honestly, in general), it's likely that your Sun's NVRAM battery is dead. This means three important things: a) your Sun will always boot up in diagnostic mode (causing it to try unsuccessfully to boot fro
m the network), b) your IDPROM information will be invalid, and c) any changes you make to settings will be cleared
 after you turn your machine off. This is a guide all about network booting and, with an invalid ethernet address, you can't do a lot. So, with that in mind, let's look at the makeup of the IDPROM data:
\par }\trowd \trgaph108\trleft-108 \clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf15 \cellx903\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf15 \cellx8460 \pard \qc\sb100\sa100\intbl {\b\f4 Byte(s)\cell Contents\cell }\pard \intbl {
\f4 \row }\trowd \trgaph108\trleft-108 \clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf15 \cellx903\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf15 \cellx8460 \pard \sb100\sa100\intbl {\f4 0\cell Always 01\cell }\pard \intbl {
\f4 \row }\pard \sb100\sa100\intbl {\f4 1\cell 
First byte of hostid (machine type - for ease of use you can use real-machine-type, but that may not work on older boot proms. DO NOT SET THIS TO A MACHINE TYPE THAT IS NOT YOUR MACHINE'S TYPE!! If you are unsure, please refer to the machine type ID table
 below (A-ii).)\cell }\pard \intbl {\f4 \row }\pard \sb100\sa100\intbl {\f4 2-7\cell 
6 byte ethernet address (first three bytes are the OUI (Oragnizationally Unique Identifier), if you are unsure of what your OUI is, please refer to the OUI table below (A-iV).)\cell }\pard \intbl {\f4 \row }\pard \sb100\sa100\intbl {\f4 8-b\cell 
Date of manufacture (all 0s, doesn't matter)\cell }\pard \intbl {\f4 \row }\pard \sb100\sa100\intbl {\f4 c\cell Second byte of hostid\cell }\pard \intbl {\f4 \row }\pard \sb100\sa100\intbl {\f4 d\cell Third byte of hostid\cell }\pard \intbl {\f4 \row 
}\pard \sb100\sa100\intbl {\f4 e\cell Fourth byte of hostid\cell }\pard \intbl {\f4 \row }\trowd \trgaph108\trleft-108 \clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf15 \cellx903\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf15 \clbrdrb
\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf15 \cellx8460 \pard \sb100\sa100\intbl {\f4 f\cell IDPROM checksum (bitwise xor of bytes 0-e)\cell }\pard \intbl {\f4 \row }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f4 
\par }\pard \sb199\sa199 {\b\i\f4\fs36 A-ii. A crash course in IDPROM programming}{\b\f4\fs36 
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f4 To program your ethernet address to 8:0:20:c0:ff:ee and your hostid to 80coffee on a sun4m machine based on the table above:
\par }\pard \tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656 {\f11\fs20 1 0 mkp
\par real-machine-type 1 mkp
\par 8 2 mkp
\par 0 3 mkp
\par 20 4 mkp
\par c0 5 mkp
\par ff 6 mkp
\par ee 7 mkp
\par 0 8 mkp
\par 0 9 mkp
\par 0 a mkp
\par 0 b mkp
\par c0 c mkp
\par ff d mkp
\par ee e mkp
\par 0 f 0 do i idprom@ xor loop f mkp
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f4 If you're on a SPARCserver 1000, type }{\f11\fs20 update-system-idprom}{\f4  at the "ok" prompt.
\par There is also another way to set the idprom on some revisions of the OBP: }{\f11\fs20 8 0 20 c0 ff ee 80c0ffee mkpl}{\f4  and hitting Return followed by hitting CTRL+D followed by CTRL+R. If it gives no output and returns you to the }{\f11\fs20 ok}{\f4 
 prompt, it has succeeded. If it shows a Sun Microsystems copyright, it has failed. This is sometimes due to the idprom not being invalidated before running the command. If this is the case, run }{\f11\fs20 f idprom@ 1 xor f mkp}{\f4 
 and hit Return, then trying the invocation again. If that still doesn't work, then try the method listed above.
\par Reset the machine with the }{\f11\fs20 reset}{\f4  command and it should reboot with the new ethernet address and hostid.
