B-iii. Useful OpenBoot settings}{\b\f4\fs36 
\par }\pard \sb240\sa240 {\b\f4\fs28 B-iii-a. diag-switch?
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f4 
This determines if the system goes through an extended self-test during startup. It also sets the default boot device to the network by default while this setting is set to true. It will be set to true by default every time you turn on your system on if t
he timekeeper battery is dead. I would recommend setting this to false to shorten the time it takes to subsequently reboot.
\par }\pard \sb240\sa240 {\b\f4\fs28 B-iii-b. auto-boot?
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f4 This determines if the system tries to auto-boot u
pon startup. This is set to true by default. It's a matter of personal preference whether it's enabled or not. I prefer to boot to the OpenBoot prompt and then choose my boot device from there. Be aware this setting will be largely inconsequential when yo
u cold boot the system if the timekeeper battery is dead.
\par }\pard \sb240\sa240 {\b\f4\fs28 B-iii-c. boot-device
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f4 
This controls what the default setting is when the boot command is issued with no arguments. The default is for it to try booting from disk first and then from the network. Like auto-boot? It's a largely inconsequential setting if your timekeeper battery 
is dead.
\par }\pard \sb240\sa240 {\b\f4\fs28 B-iii-d. output-device
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f4 I put this here because sometimes you don't want the default output device to be what the firmware defaults to, or you want to change settings for the default output device. This setting allows you to specify the default outp
ut device (generally screen or ttya by default depending on if you have a keyboard attached or not) and any settings for said output device (I set my framebuffer to run at 1024x768x60 using this setting, for instance).
\par }\pard \sb199\sa199 {\b\i\f4\fs36 B-iv. Working with iso files}{\b\f4\fs36 
\par }\pard \sb240\sa240 {\b\f4\fs28 B-iv-a. Using lofiadm to mount iso images. (Solaris)
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f4 In some versions of Solaris (I've tested with Solaris 10, but it may exist in other versions as well) there exists the lofiadm utility. This exists as a way to work with loopback files (disc/k image files). For 
the purposes of this guide, we will be looking at using it to mount CD images.
\par You can invoke the lofiadm utility with the following:
\par }{\f11\fs20 # lofiadm -a /path/to/image.iso}{\f4 
\par This will add the iso file as a loopback filesystem on the first available lofi device. You can also specify a lofi device, like so (where n is the lofi device number):
\par }{\f11\fs20 # lofiadm -a /path/to/image.iso /dev/lofi/n}{\f4 
\par The output of either of these commands will return the lofi device that the iso file was added to. You can then mount the device like so (where n is the lofi device number):
\par }{\f11\fs20 # mount -F hsfs -o ro /dev/lofi/n /mnt}{\f4 
\par You can also combine both commands together:
\par }{\f11\fs20 # mount -F hsfs -o ro $(lofiadm -a /path/to/image.iso) /mnt}{\f4 
\par }\pard \sb240\sa240 {\b\f4\fs28 B-iv-b. Using vnconfig to mount iso images. (NetBSD/OpenBSD)
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f4 To mount cdrom images in NetBSD and OpenBSD, you will want to use vnconfig. To create a device file that is linked to your image, run the following:
\par }{\f11\fs20 # vnconfig vnd0 /path/to/image.iso}{\f4 
\par Then it will be possible to mount this device as you would a normal cdrom:
\par }{\f11\fs20 # mount -t cd9660 /dev/vnd0a /mnt}{\f4 
\par Once that is finished, you may work with the image as you would any other cdrom filesystem.
\par }\pard \sb199\sa199 {\b\i\f4\fs36 B-v. Naming your tftp boot file.}{\b\f4\fs36 
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f4 This is a short and simple guide for how to name your Sun's tftp boot file, which needs to be named in a specific way.
\par Sun workstations require the tftp boot file to be named for their IP address represented in hexadecimal followed by their platform group.
\par First, you need to determine your IP address in hexadecimal. This can be done with the bc command.
\par Secondly, you need to determine your platform group. See Appendix B-i for more information. For the sake of this demonstration, I'll be choosing the sun4u platform group.
\par }\pard \tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656 {\f11\fs20 # bc
\par obase=16
\par 192
\par C0
\par 168
\par A8
\par 0
\par 0
\par 10
\par A
\par }\pard \sb100\sa100 {\f4 Thirdly, you must symbolically link the boot file to your /tftpboot directory.
\par }{\f11\fs20 # ln -s /path/to/boot/file /tftpboot/C0A8000A.SUN4U}{\f4 
\par }\pard \sb199\sa199 {\b\f4\fs36 Appendix C: Cited works and further reading
